-- Q1: medium / L2 — EPS for discontinued operations (separate presentation)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverly Industries reports income from continuing operations of $720,000 and a loss from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $180,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025. Waverly has 200,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding and no preferred stock. What is the EPS amount that Waverly must report for discontinued operations?', '["$(0.63)", "$(1.80)", "$2.70", "$(0.90)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, an entity must present EPS for discontinued operations either on the face of the income statement or in the notes. The per-share amount is calculated as the discontinued operations loss ($180,000) divided by weighted-average shares (200,000), yielding $(0.90). Choice C ($2.70) incorrectly uses total income from continuing operations in the numerator instead of the discontinued operations amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of simple vs. complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what distinguishes a complex capital structure from a simple capital structure?', '["A complex capital structure includes potentially dilutive securities such as convertible bonds or stock options", "A complex capital structure has more than one class of common stock outstanding", "A complex capital structure requires use of the direct method for EPS computation", "A complex capital structure has total equity exceeding $10 million"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS, such as convertible debt, convertible preferred stock, options, or warrants. Choice B is incorrect because having multiple classes of common stock alone does not define a complex capital structure without potentially dilutive securities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Restricted stock units in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Montclair Corp. has net income of $900,000 and 300,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding during 2025. Montclair granted 12,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) that vest over three years. During 2025, the average unrecognized compensation cost is $180,000 and the average market price per share is $60. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares from the RSUs are included in diluted EPS?', '["12,000", "9,000", "3,000", "6,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28, unvested RSUs are treated similarly to options under the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds equal the average unrecognized compensation cost ($180,000). Shares assumed repurchased = $180,000 / $60 = 3,000. Incremental shares = 12,000 - 3,000 = 9,000. Choice A (12,000) incorrectly includes all RSUs without subtracting the shares assumed repurchased from unrecognized compensation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether actual mid-year conversion changes EPS treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Drexel Manufacturing has 200,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025, and net income of $1,200,000. On July 1, 2025, holders of $500,000 face value, 8% convertible bonds (each $1,000 bond converts into 20 shares) exercise their conversion rights. The tax rate is 25%. The CFO must determine how to handle these converted shares for both basic and diluted EPS. Evaluate the correct treatment of the converted bonds.', '["Include 5,000 shares for 6/12 of the year in basic EPS; for diluted EPS, assume conversion as of January 1 for the full year", "Include 10,000 shares for the full year in basic EPS; exclude from diluted EPS since conversion already occurred", "Include 5,000 shares for 6/12 in basic EPS; no adjustment needed for diluted EPS since the bonds were actually converted", "Include 10,000 shares for 6/12 of the year in basic EPS; for diluted EPS, treat as if converted on July 1"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when convertible securities are actually converted during the period, the resulting common shares are included in basic EPS from the conversion date (10,000 shares x 6/12 = 5,000 weighted shares). For diluted EPS, the shares are treated as if converted at the beginning of the period, and the numerator is adjusted for interest saved net of tax. Choice C incorrectly omits the diluted EPS adjustment required for the pre-conversion period.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Anti-dilution sequencing (most dilutive first)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prescott Holdings has basic EPS of $5.00 based on net income of $750,000 and 150,000 weighted-average shares. The company has three potentially dilutive securities: (1) stock options adding 8,000 incremental shares and $0 to the numerator, (2) convertible bonds adding $24,000 (after tax) and 12,000 shares, and (3) convertible preferred adding $45,000 in dividends and 7,500 shares. Evaluate which securities should be included in diluted EPS after applying the proper sequencing rules under ASC 260.', '["All three securities; each individually reduces EPS below $5.00", "Options only; both convertible securities are antidilutive", "Options and convertible bonds only; convertible preferred is antidilutive when tested in sequence", "Options and convertible preferred only; convertible bonds are antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, potentially dilutive securities must be tested in order from most dilutive to least dilutive. Options ($0/8,000 = $0.00 per share) are most dilutive, reducing EPS to $4.75. Convertible bonds ($24,000/12,000 = $2.00 per share) are next, reducing EPS to $4.56. Convertible preferred ($45,000/7,500 = $6.00 per share) exceeds the running EPS of $4.56, so it is antidilutive and excluded. Choice A incorrectly includes all securities without sequential testing.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Effect of stock repurchase on weighted-average shares and EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fieldstone Corp. reports net income of $750,000 with 250,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On July 1, 2025, Fieldstone repurchases 50,000 shares as treasury stock. If net income remains constant, analyze how the July 1 repurchase affects basic EPS compared to what EPS would have been without the repurchase.', '["EPS increases from $3.00 to $3.33 because the reduced weighted-average share count raises per-share earnings", "EPS decreases from $3.00 to $2.73 because treasury stock reduces the numerator", "EPS remains $3.00 because treasury stock repurchases do not affect weighted-average shares", "EPS increases from $3.00 to $3.75 because repurchased shares are removed for the full year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares repurchased are removed from the weighted-average calculation from the repurchase date. Without the repurchase, EPS = $750,000 / 250,000 = $3.00. With the repurchase, weighted-average shares = 250,000 - (50,000 x 6/12) = 225,000, yielding EPS of $750,000 / 225,000 = $3.33. Choice D ($3.75) incorrectly removes the 50,000 repurchased shares for the full year (200,000 shares) rather than weighting from the repurchase date.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Convertible debt issued mid-year partial-year if-converted
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On April 1, 2025, Blackwell Corp. issues $1,000,000 face value, 6% convertible bonds at par. Each $1,000 bond converts into 25 shares. Blackwell has net income of $850,000, 200,000 weighted-average basic shares, and a 25% tax rate. The bonds were outstanding for nine months. Evaluate the proper diluted EPS calculation, considering the partial-year outstanding period.', '["$4.25", "$3.70", "$4.50", "$4.04"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, convertible securities issued during the period are included in diluted EPS from the issue date. The numerator adds back interest net of tax: $1,000,000 x 6% x 9/12 x (1 - 0.25) = $33,750. Shares added: 25,000 x 9/12 = 18,750. Diluted EPS = ($850,000 + $33,750) / (200,000 + 18,750) = $883,750 / 218,750 = $4.04. Choice A ($4.25) incorrectly uses basic EPS without the conversion adjustment.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Definition of weighted-average shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the purpose of using weighted-average common shares outstanding in the EPS calculation?', '["To adjust for inflation in the share price during the reporting period", "To reflect the time period during which shares were actually outstanding and available to earn income", "To eliminate the effect of treasury stock on total equity", "To comply with SEC reporting requirements for market capitalization"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average shares are used because the number of shares outstanding may change during the period. Weighting by the fraction of the period each share was outstanding ensures EPS reflects the actual capital available to generate earnings. Choice C is incorrect because while treasury stock reduces shares outstanding, the purpose of weighting is to capture timing, not to adjust equity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: easy / L2 — Warrants using treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartley Inc. has 150,000 weighted-average shares outstanding and net income of $600,000. Hartley has 20,000 warrants outstanding with an exercise price of $30. The average market price during 2025 was $50. How many incremental shares result from the warrants under the treasury stock method?', '["12,000", "6,000", "8,000", "20,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-23, the treasury stock method assumes warrants are exercised at the beginning of the period. Proceeds = 20,000 x $30 = $600,000. Shares repurchased = $600,000 / $50 = 12,000. Incremental shares = 20,000 - 12,000 = 8,000. Choice D (20,000) incorrectly includes all warrant shares without subtracting the shares assumed repurchased with the proceeds.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Effect of retroactive stock split on prior-period EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Aldrich Technologies reported basic EPS of $4.80 for the year ended December 31, 2024, based on 250,000 weighted-average shares. On March 15, 2025, before the 2024 financial statements were issued, Aldrich declared a 2-for-1 stock split. Analyze how this stock split affects the previously reported 2024 EPS when presented in the 2025 comparative financial statements.', '["2024 EPS remains $4.80 because the split occurred in a subsequent period", "2024 EPS is restated to $2.40 only if the split is disclosed in the notes", "2024 EPS is adjusted prospectively beginning in 2025", "2024 EPS must be retroactively restated to $2.40 using 500,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits and stock dividends occurring after the balance sheet date but before issuance of financial statements must be given retroactive effect in computing EPS for all periods presented. The 2024 weighted-average shares become 500,000, and EPS is restated to $4.80 x (1/2) = $2.40. Choice A incorrectly treats the split as a subsequent event that does not require EPS restatement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Written put options increasing denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Greystone Corp. has net income of $500,000, 100,000 weighted-average shares outstanding, and a written put option obligating it to repurchase 10,000 shares at $60. The average market price during 2025 was $40. The CFO asks whether this put option affects diluted EPS. Evaluate the proper treatment under ASC 260.', '["The put option is dilutive and adds incremental shares because the exercise price exceeds the market price, requiring the reverse treasury stock method", "The put option is antidilutive and excluded because put options never affect diluted EPS", "The put option is dilutive and reduces the numerator by the premium received", "The put option is antidilutive because the market price exceeds the exercise price"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. The entity would repurchase 10,000 shares at $60 ($600,000) but could buy only 10,000 shares at $40 on the open market ($400,000). The excess $200,000 / $40 = 5,000 additional shares must be added to the denominator. Choice D reverses the dilution test: for puts, dilution occurs when exercise price exceeds market price, not the opposite.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Numerator for basic EPS with preferred dividends
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What amount is used as the numerator when calculating basic earnings per share for common shareholders?', '["Net income plus preferred dividends", "Net income minus preferred dividends", "Operating income minus preferred dividends", "Net income minus common dividends declared"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, the numerator for basic EPS is net income (or loss) attributable to common shareholders, which requires subtracting preferred dividends (whether declared or, for cumulative preferred, accumulated). Choice D is incorrect because common dividends declared do not reduce the EPS numerator; they are a distribution of earnings, not a deduction from income available to common shareholders.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Participating securities and two-class method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summerlin Inc. has net income of $1,000,000, 200,000 common shares, and 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock that receives a $2 base dividend plus shares proportionally in any additional dividends declared to common shareholders. Summerlin declared total dividends of $500,000 for 2025. Analyze how the participating preferred stock affects the basic EPS calculation.', '["Basic EPS is $5.00 because participating preferred dividends are treated the same as nonparticipating preferred dividends", "Basic EPS is $4.50 because only the $100,000 base preferred dividend is subtracted from the numerator", "Undistributed earnings must be allocated between common and preferred shares using the two-class method, reducing basic EPS below $5.00", "The two-class method applies only to diluted EPS and does not affect basic EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, participating securities that share in undistributed earnings with common stock require use of the two-class method for basic EPS. Undistributed earnings ($1,000,000 - $500,000 = $500,000) are allocated between common and preferred based on participation rights, reducing the per-share amount available to common shareholders below $5.00. Choice A incorrectly ignores the participation feature and treats these as standard preferred shares.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — EPS presentation requirement on income statement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement under U.S. GAAP?', '["All entities regardless of whether they are publicly traded", "Only entities with complex capital structures", "Only entities with annual revenues exceeding $50 million", "Public entities with common stock or potential common stock traded in a public market"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for public business entities whose common stock or potential common stock is traded in a public market. Nonpublic entities are not required to present EPS data, though they may do so voluntarily. Choice A is incorrect because private companies are explicitly excluded from the mandatory EPS presentation requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Shares issued for acquisition mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On August 1, 2025, Pemberton Corp. issues 40,000 shares of common stock to acquire Larkin Enterprises in a business combination. Pemberton had 300,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025, and no other share transactions during the year. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for 2025?', '["316,667", "340,000", "320,000", "300,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued during the period are weighted from the date of issuance. The 40,000 acquisition shares are weighted for 5/12 of the year (August 1 through December 31). Weighted-average = 300,000 + (40,000 x 5/12) = 300,000 + 16,667 = 316,667. Choice D (300,000) incorrectly excludes the newly issued shares entirely from the weighted-average calculation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Impact of stock compensation expense on EPS analysis
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Industries granted 15,000 stock options with a fair value of $450,000, vesting over three years. In Year 1, Oakmont recognizes $150,000 of stock compensation expense, reducing net income. The options have an exercise price of $35, and the average market price is $50. Analyze how the stock compensation expense and the options interact in computing both basic and diluted EPS.', '["Stock compensation expense has no effect on EPS because it is a noncash charge that is added back", "Stock compensation expense reduces the basic EPS numerator through lower net income, while the options also increase the diluted EPS denominator via the treasury stock method", "The options reduce basic EPS but have no further effect on diluted EPS since the expense is already recognized", "Stock compensation expense increases basic EPS by reducing shares outstanding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28 and ASC 718, stock compensation expense flows through net income, reducing the basic EPS numerator. Separately, the outstanding options create incremental shares under the treasury stock method for diluted EPS. These are two distinct effects: one on the numerator (expense) and one on the denominator (dilutive shares). Choice A incorrectly assumes the noncash nature of the expense means it is excluded from net income for EPS purposes.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — What happens to dilutive securities when there is a net loss
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, how are potentially dilutive securities treated when a company reports a net loss from continuing operations?', '["Potentially dilutive securities are still included to show the maximum potential dilution", "Only stock options are excluded; convertible securities are still included", "All potentially dilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS because their inclusion would be antidilutive", "Diluted EPS equals zero regardless of the number of dilutive securities"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, when an entity reports a loss from continuing operations, including any potentially dilutive securities in the denominator would reduce the loss per share, which is antidilutive. Therefore all potentially dilutive securities are excluded, and diluted EPS equals basic EPS. Choice A incorrectly includes dilutive securities despite the antidilutive effect during a net loss period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Contingently issuable shares when conditions are NOT met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeline Corp. has an agreement to issue 25,000 additional shares to former owners of an acquired business if cumulative revenues exceed $20 million by December 31, 2025. Actual cumulative revenues through December 31, 2025, are $17.5 million. Analyze how these contingently issuable shares are treated for basic and diluted EPS in the 2025 financial statements.', '["Included in both basic and diluted EPS because the agreement exists regardless of whether the target is met", "Included in basic EPS at a probability-weighted estimate of the shares expected to vest", "Excluded from both basic and diluted EPS because the revenue condition has not been satisfied at the reporting date", "Excluded from basic EPS only; always included in diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only when all necessary conditions have been satisfied by the end of the reporting period. Since the $20 million revenue threshold was not met, the shares are excluded from basic EPS. Under ASC 260-10-45-51, they are also excluded from diluted EPS when the contingency conditions are not currently met at the reporting date. Choice A incorrectly includes shares regardless of whether vesting conditions are satisfied.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted method numerator adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestwood Industries has net income of $640,000, 160,000 weighted-average common shares, and 8,000 shares of $50 par, 10% convertible preferred stock, each convertible into 5 common shares. All preferred dividends were declared and paid. Under the if-converted method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$3.20", "$3.60", "$4.00", "$2.80"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock adds back preferred dividends to the numerator and adds the converted shares to the denominator. Preferred dividends = 8,000 x $50 x 10% = $40,000. Basic EPS numerator = $640,000 - $40,000 = $600,000. Diluted numerator = $640,000 (dividends added back). Diluted shares = 160,000 + 40,000 = 200,000. Diluted EPS = $640,000 / 200,000 = $3.20. Choice B ($3.60) incorrectly fails to add the converted shares to the denominator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Weighted-average for shares issued for services
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Belmont Consulting had 100,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On May 1, 2025, Belmont issued 6,000 shares to an attorney as compensation for legal services. No other share transactions occurred during the year. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for 2025?', '["106,000", "104,000", "103,000", "100,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued for services are included in the weighted-average from the date of issuance. The 6,000 shares are weighted for 8/12 of the year (May 1 through December 31): 6,000 x 8/12 = 4,000. Weighted-average = 100,000 + 4,000 = 104,000. Choice A (106,000) incorrectly includes all 6,000 shares as if they were outstanding for the full year.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Dual presentation requirement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does ASC 260 require a public entity with a complex capital structure to present on the face of the income statement?', '["Only diluted EPS for net income", "Basic EPS for income from continuing operations and diluted EPS for net income", "Both basic EPS and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income", "Only basic EPS with diluted EPS disclosed in the notes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, public entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and net income. Choice A is incorrect because omitting basic EPS violates the dual presentation requirement mandated by ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: hard / L3 — Stock appreciation rights in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornfield Systems grants 10,000 stock appreciation rights (SARs) that may be settled in shares. The SARs have a strike price of $25, the average market price during 2025 is $50, and the average unrecognized compensation cost is $50,000. Thornfield has net income of $800,000 and 200,000 weighted-average shares. Determine the incremental shares from the SARs for diluted EPS and explain how share-settled SARs differ from cash-settled SARs in the EPS calculation.', '["4,000 incremental shares; cash-settled SARs are excluded from the diluted EPS denominator because they do not result in share issuance", "10,000 incremental shares; both share-settled and cash-settled SARs are included in diluted EPS", "5,000 incremental shares; cash-settled SARs are also included using the treasury stock method", "0 incremental shares; SARs never affect diluted EPS until they are exercised"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28, share-settled SARs use the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds include the exercise price and unrecognized compensation cost: (10,000 x $25) + $50,000 = $300,000. Shares repurchased at average market price = $300,000 / $50 = 6,000. Incremental shares = 10,000 - 6,000 = 4,000. Cash-settled SARs create a liability, not potential shares, and are excluded from diluted EPS. Choice B incorrectly includes all 10,000 SARs without applying the treasury stock method.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Effect of mid-year stock dividend on prior shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinehurst Corp. had 180,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025, and issued 20,000 new shares on April 1, 2025. On October 1, 2025, Pinehurst declared a 20% stock dividend. Analyze how the stock dividend affects the weighted-average shares calculation for 2025.', '["The stock dividend only applies to shares outstanding on October 1, yielding weighted-average shares of 215,000", "The stock dividend is weighted for 3/12 of the year and added to the pre-dividend weighted-average", "The stock dividend has no effect on weighted-average shares because no cash was exchanged", "The stock dividend is applied retroactively to all shares outstanding before the dividend date, resulting in weighted-average shares of 234,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends are applied retroactively to all shares outstanding prior to the dividend. January 1 shares: 180,000 x 1.20 = 216,000 (full year). April 1 shares: 20,000 x 1.20 = 24,000 (for 9/12). Weighted-average = 216,000 + (24,000 x 9/12) = 216,000 + 18,000 = 234,000. Choice B incorrectly treats the stock dividend as a new issuance that should be time-weighted from the declaration date.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the treasury stock method used for in calculating diluted earnings per share?', '["Calculating the effect of treasury stock repurchases on basic EPS", "Determining the incremental shares from stock options and warrants by assuming exercise and share repurchase at the average market price", "Measuring the dilutive impact of convertible bonds on the EPS numerator", "Adjusting the EPS denominator for shares held as treasury stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes that options and warrants are exercised at the beginning of the period and that the proceeds are used to repurchase shares at the average market price. The difference between shares issued and shares repurchased represents incremental dilutive shares. Choice C is incorrect because convertible bonds use the if-converted method, not the treasury stock method.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Evaluate mandatorily redeemable preferred shares in EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Evergreen Financial has 100,000 common shares outstanding and net income of $750,000. Evergreen also has 5,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock (classified as a liability under ASC 480) with a $6 annual dividend. The CFO is debating whether to deduct the $30,000 preferred dividend from the basic EPS numerator. Evaluate the correct treatment of these mandatorily redeemable preferred dividends in the EPS calculation.', '["The $30,000 dividend is recognized as interest expense in net income, so no further deduction from the EPS numerator is required", "The $30,000 dividend must be deducted from net income in the EPS numerator regardless of classification", "The mandatorily redeemable preferred shares are excluded from EPS calculations entirely", "The $30,000 dividend is added back to net income because the preferred stock is a liability"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 480-10-25-4, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability, and its dividends are recognized as interest expense in the income statement, reducing net income. Since the charge already flows through net income, no separate deduction from the EPS numerator is needed. Choice B incorrectly double-counts by deducting the dividend from net income when it has already been expensed as interest.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — What period EPS uses (average vs. end-of-period shares)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which share count is used as the denominator in the basic EPS calculation?', '["Common shares outstanding at the end of the reporting period", "Common shares outstanding at the beginning of the reporting period", "The maximum number of common shares outstanding at any point during the period", "Weighted-average common shares outstanding during the period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the denominator for basic EPS is the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding during the reporting period. This approach captures the time-weighted impact of share issuances and repurchases throughout the period. Choice A is incorrect because using the end-of-period share count would not reflect shares that were outstanding for only part of the year.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Numerator adjustment for convertible bonds net of tax
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Industries has net income of $1,100,000, 250,000 weighted-average shares, and $2,000,000 face value of 5% convertible bonds (each $1,000 bond converts into 15 shares). The tax rate is 25%. Under the if-converted method, what is the adjusted numerator for diluted EPS?', '["$1,200,000", "$1,025,000", "$1,175,000", "$1,100,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back interest expense on convertible bonds, net of tax, to the numerator. Interest = $2,000,000 x 5% = $100,000. Tax savings lost = $100,000 x 25% = $25,000. After-tax add-back = $100,000 - $25,000 = $75,000. Adjusted numerator = $1,100,000 + $75,000 = $1,175,000. Choice A ($1,200,000) incorrectly adds back the full $100,000 interest without adjusting for taxes.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Year-to-date EPS for interim reporting
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sterling Corp. reports quarterly results as a public entity. For the six months ended June 30, 2025, Sterling has net income of $360,000 and weighted-average shares of 120,000. For Q3 (July 1 to September 30), net income is $210,000 and weighted-average shares are 130,000 (due to a July share issuance). Under ASC 260, what is the year-to-date basic EPS for the nine months ended September 30, 2025?', '["$3.00", "$4.38", "$4.62", "$4.75"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, year-to-date EPS for interim periods uses the cumulative year-to-date weighted-average shares and cumulative net income. Year-to-date net income = $360,000 + $210,000 = $570,000. Year-to-date weighted-average shares = (120,000 x 6/9) + (130,000 x 3/9) = 80,000 + 43,333 = 123,333. EPS = $570,000 / 123,333 = $4.62. Choice B ($4.38) incorrectly uses only the Q3 share count (130,000) for the entire nine-month period ($570,000 / 130,000).', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Options exercised mid-year — split treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Haverford Corp. has 250,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On June 1, 2025, holders exercise 10,000 stock options at $20 per share when the market price is $55. The average market price for 2025 is $50. For diluted EPS, how are the exercised options treated for the period before and after the exercise date?', '["The 10,000 options are included in diluted EPS for the full year using the treasury stock method", "For January through May, the treasury stock method is applied using 10,000 options; from June through December, the 10,000 shares are included as actual outstanding shares in basic EPS", "The 10,000 shares are included in basic EPS for the full year because options were exercised", "The options are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS after exercise"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-26, when options are exercised during the period, the treasury stock method applies for the pre-exercise period (January through May), and the resulting actual shares are included in basic EPS from the exercise date (June through December). This split treatment ensures diluted EPS reflects both the potential dilution before exercise and the actual dilution after. Choice A incorrectly applies the treasury stock method for the entire year, ignoring the actual conversion that occurred.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — EPS disclosure reconciliation requirement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Corp. presents both basic and diluted EPS on its income statement. The company''s controller is preparing the EPS footnote disclosures. Under ASC 260, analyze what reconciliation information must be provided in the notes to the financial statements.', '["Only a reconciliation of the denominators since the numerator is always net income", "A reconciliation of the numerators only, with the share counts disclosed on the face of the income statement", "No reconciliation is required if both basic and diluted EPS are presented on the face of the income statement", "A reconciliation of both the numerators and denominators used in the basic and diluted EPS computations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-50-1, entities must disclose a reconciliation of both the numerators and denominators of the basic and diluted EPS computations. This includes showing the effect of each class of securities (options, convertible debt, convertible preferred) on both the income available to common shareholders and the share count. Choice A is incorrect because the numerator often differs between basic and diluted EPS due to add-backs for convertible securities.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
